here it's detailed, kinda abstract; on git it's short, concrete: [github/scclie/sccl_nix/README.md](https://github.com/scclie/sccl_nix)

# ? ~~the f*&k for~~ why

back then, after 6 years of using only arch - i didn't get it either...

for a long time i had this old-fart opinion lodged in me about stuff like this: they've spawned another mainstream distro, like fedora, manjaro and other cachys.

and then i wanted to install it... during and after, my world flipped upside down:

a bunch of problems that came up on normal distros - got solved in no time. i hadn't even thought of them as problems before - over years of using standard distros you reach a consensus, just get used to how you gotta work in them
to make everything fucking great, but that fucking great and this fucking great are two completely different fucking greats.

on normal linux distros you can't reproduce a particular system state, since all the spaghetti connections and stuff are just impossible to reproduce after years of using one system.
you can only rely on backups and snapshots like zfs and so on, but that's not storing the system state, just a crutch for rolling back, not a way to reproduce the same state on, say, another machine.

in nix you just describe all the packages, configs, users, services and so on - you get a complete inventory of the environment's state, with which you don't fucking need a system backup anymore to save its state - you only need to back up data (user files and stuff).

later you can replicate it onto a second machine with the same config too.

nix stores a generation as symlinks - so basically everything lives in a single `/nix/store`, in one huge pile of ~~shit~~ - so there are no dependency conflicts, you can roll back to any state (generation) and clone it wherever.

one neat feature - `nix shell` - you can throw in some apps, use them, drop/forget them - and they'll get deleted at the next garbage collector (cleaning useless junk out of `/nix/store`).


# # where nix came from

in 2006 a cool dude named Eelco Dolstra defended his PhD "[The Purely Functional Software Deployment Model](https://edolstra.github.io/pubs/phd-thesis.pdf)" at Utrecht University.

> what if a package is a pure function?
>
> input: source code + dependencies + compiler flags.
>
> output: a binary.
>
> same input = same output (on any machine)

: that's the core idea, not an unconditional guarantee, in reality not all software builds bit-for-bit identically: randomness can affect some things, a timeshatmp somewhere, etc.

in nix like every package lives in its own directory, and the path includes a hash of all inputs:

```
/nix/store/5mq2jcn36ldd97xrcnf3jg8irnfp5xqr-fish-4.0.1/
```

the hash depends on the sources, dependencies, compiler flags, compiler version - EVERYTHING.

any change - a different hash - a different path: that's why there are no dependency conflicts.

it's a completely different paradigm compared to other package managers.

the nixpkgs ecosystem is now [the world's largest package repository](https://repology.org/repositories/graphs). and all of it gets built by one hydra farm and cached in a binary cache.

actually the numbers are a bit misleading (well, more like they're literally bullshitting you with them (trying to sell it)) - loads of packages are split into a bunch of little ones. if you look at actual software - nixpkgs is smaller than even aur.

# # imperative vs declarative

as someone dumped out in some very old thought back when nix was first being dreamed up - package managers can be compared to programming languages.

there's [the article "Functional Package Management with Guix"](https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Functional_Package_Management_with_Guix) that explores this thought well, but the idea had been around before.

*too lazy to find where else, but it was!*

procedural package managers - the standard apt, yum,pacman and so on (i.e. like all imperative package managers, where you install everything by hand, or write sequential scripts for it)

declarative package managers - that's nix, and there's guix too (that's more like a functional approach, where you're mostly describing the result)

what's next? some higher-level approach, more abstract? (or a mega abstract llm-based chat interface??????? - ai problem resolver :D )

wait... [omarchy linux](https://omarchy.org/).

# # ecosystem

nixos itself only solves package manager problems, and there's a whole bunch more stuff you want to make declarative.

## ## flakes

experimental features since 2021... actually almost everyone uses it, you could've called it stable ages ago.

it gives you two *conveniences*:
- dependency versioning through `flake.lock`
- a common input output format

you could pin nixpkgs versions and get by before flakes too. flakes just put it into a more standardized structure.

in my config the flake auto-discovers hosts from folders - drop in `hosts/<hostname>/`, and it picks it up in `nixosConfigurations.<hostname>` by itself.

## ## home-manager

[github.com/nix-community/home-manager](https://github.com/nix-community/home-manager) - lets you manage the user environment declaratively.

dotfiles used to be handled with gnu stow or a bare git repo, and home-manager lets you do the same thing, but nix-way: with options, dependencies and immutability.

integrates with a nixos module. One `nixos-rebuild switch` rebuilds both the system and the user environment.

## ## disko

[github.com/nix-community/disko](https://github.com/nix-community/disko) - in nix it's described as code, except disk partitioning - that's done by hand during installation. disko fixes this sad omission.

just write the disk partitioning config once, instead of wanking around with `gparted` every time
if hosts have identical disks - you can replicate that too no problem, maybe with some small tweaks.

## ## sops-nix

[github.com/mic92/sops-nix](https://github.com/mic92/sops-nix) - lets you store secrets (ssh keys, passwords, gpg) right in the repo, even in plain sight. they get decrypted at build time and mounted in the right places.

there's [a little article](https://lgug2z.com/articles/handling-secrets-in-nixos-an-overview/) about *smart* secret management - i try to follow it.

# # the scaling problem

nix is a cool tool, but when building an architecture with it you can turn everything into a dump full of exceptions and stuff just like anywhere else.

there are approaches to avoid that:

- [flake-parts](https://github.com/hercules-ci/flake-parts) - a module system for flakes, lets you split the config into reusable modules. good for big projects, but adds its own abstraction layer.
- [snowfall-lib](https://github.com/snowfallorg/lib) - a library for organizing nix configs with auto-discovery. similar to what i do, but with its own philosophy.
- you can just wing it - with one or two systems it doesn't hurt much.

# # sccl_nix architecture
*(well, what i'm doing, fuck knows how right it is)*


my dotfiles are split into three entities.

- hosts - hardware and system stuff: disko, gpu drivers, network, system packages - everything you can't sync between different machines. auto-discovered by the flake from `hosts/`.
- profiles - a user with their zoo of apps, shell and user-level junk. one host can have as many profiles as you want. imported into the host config.
- shared - a base for everyone. various core apps and services imported for everyone.

currently two three hosts: sacculos(desktop, AMD) and aero15laptop(laptop, NVIDIA), laerandr(server)

two profiles: **paper** (main, with the whole zoo) and **bootstrap** (minimal, just vim, nmtui and ssh for installation from a usb stick).


as an example of actual use.

