## ## sccl.*

The main feature that keeps the config from sliding into a dump of conditionals.

ALL options are defined in `core.nix`, and they're all `false` by default. Each module gates itself with `lib.mkIf config.sccl.<feature>.enable`.

```nix
{ config, lib, ... }:
{
  config = lib.mkIf config.sccl.zapret.enable {
    services.zapret = { ... };
  };
}
```

full list of options *(at the time of writing, dunno if i'll update it)*:

```
sccl.ui.enable           # Nord theme + greetd
sccl.audio.enable        # pipewire
sccl.bluetooth.enable    # bluetooth + blueman
sccl.net.enable          # networkmanager + proxy + firewall
sccl.automount.enable    # udisks2 auto-mounting usb drives
sccl.secrets.enable      # sops-nix encrypted secrets
sccl.zapret.enable       # DPI bypass
sccl.flclashx.enable     # FlClashX GUI for proxies
sccl.playground.enable   # Docker + dev tools
sccl.chaotic.enable      # chaotic-nyx repo
sccl.nix-ld.enable       # nix-ld for non-nixpkgs binaries
sccl.bootstrap           # bool: minimal installation mode
```

The host config literally looks like this:

```nix
sccl = {
  ui.enable = true;
  audio.enable = true;
  net.enable = true;
  automount.enable = true;    # desktop only
  zapret.enable = true;       # desktop only
  playground.enable = true;   # desktop only
  nix-ld.enable = true;
};
```

*there are also server options, enabled the same way, even more of them, not gonna list them*

to add a new host you just copy the whole folder, edit `configuration.nix`, generate `hardware-configuration.nix`, and edit `disko.nix`- and the flake picks it up by itself.

the bootstrap profile solves the problem of installation on weak hardware (it's a crutch, but it's more convenient for me)

i mostly use [nixos-anywhere](https://github.com/nix-community/nixos-anywhere)

## ## Layout

The layout is parameterized through flake arguments (`specialArgs`).

On sacculos there's an ergo split with vial firmware - `us,rulemak_vial`. On aero15laptop - `colemak_dh,rulemak`. one `mkHost` in `flake.nix`, and every layer (system keyboard, xkb, wm) gets the right layout.

at the wm (niri) level the layout is passed as a parameter too: colemak-caws on the desktop, colemak-dh-wide on the laptop. handy for different keyboards with different firmware and different layouts on different machines.

# # the catch?

- you literally gotta learn a new language - nix
- there are docs, the docs are cool, you learn a lot by poking around and reading other people's configs. literally [How to Learn Nix](https://ianthehenry.com/posts/how-to-learn-nix/) - holy shit that's a lotta letters they typed :skull:
- dicking around with nix-ld/patchelf when bringing in your own software, if it tries looking for dependencies at specific paths.
- build time: everything takes ages to build, not as long as gentoo, some stuff comes from cache, flakes can also slow the build down a lot. minor changes go through fairly quickly.
- nix doesn't solve data versioning.
- overkill for simple things
- with enough skill you can shit all over this too.

i don't know why i use it, i enjoy it.
